<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 6: </span>Accuracy (and RMSE for the <span id="S2.T6.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">variance</span> task) of <math id="S2.T6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k-ary" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-ary</annotation></semantics></math> Janossy pooling approximations with the same input dimension as <math id="S2.T6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1</annotation></semantics></math> under distinct tasks. The <span id="S2.T6.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">method</span> column refers to the method used to deal with the sum over all permutations. <span id="S2.T6.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Infr sample</span> refers to the number of permutations sampled at test time to estimate equation <a href="#S2.E11" title="Equation 11 ‣ Remark 2.2 (Inference). ‣ Inference. ‣ 2.3 Tractability through Permutation Sampling ‣ 2 Janossy Pooling ‣ Janossy Pooling: Learning Deep Permutation-invariant Functions for Variable-size Inputs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> for methods learned with <math id="S2.T6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>-SGD. <math id="S2.T6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to DeepSets. <math id="S2.T6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tanh" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>tanh</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tanh</annotation></semantics></math> activations are used with the MLP. Standard deviations computed over 15 runs are shown in parentheses.</figcaption>
